      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Chronic pancreatitis has been difficult to treat because the origin, pathophysiologic mechanisms and causes of unrelenting pain are so poorly understood. Furthermore, the pharmacologic agents often employed in other diseases with pain appear to be ineffective in many cases. The conservative management of chronic pancreatitis aims at (1) limiting progression and complications of the disease; (2) replacing lost exocrine and endocrine function; and (3) pain control. Thus, life style changes such as cessation of alcohol consumption and tobacco smoking, trials of pancreatic enzymes, treatment of duct obstruction and pseudocysts, and surgical therapies are currently employed. The good news is that the understanding of the underlying pathophysiological mechanisms is now advancing rapidly, and hopefully patient-specific and highly effective therapies will become available in the near future."},
